im period we  were running a consulted ballot for  industrial action so we had 98 of our  members voted in favor of strike action  on the 74 turnout so we had a very  strong mandate when we reopened the  negotiations I think the companies saw  that their Workforce was serious about  getting um their Fair reward as they  contribute to massive profits and then  we got to a deal where we believe we you  know hit a genuine position that we  could agree to recommend it to our  membership via our ballot which was  taken to the plant in a referendum and  our members accepted it by 74 on an 84  turnout so it was a positive result so I  mean how close did it actually come to  strike action I mean it sounds like  obviously the members would have would  have gone that far have had it been  necessary it doesn't sound like it it  really reached that point from what you  said well yeah I think ultimately you  know we for us it's important that we  ensure that we have a a cast iron steer  from our members is to as to where they  want to go next should we not be able to  to meet their demands you know so  certainly I think we we had everything  ready if the company weren't going to  make an improved offer then we were  going to serve notice of a strike ballot  and I've no doubt that our members would  have responded with an overwhelming  mandate to proceed to to strike action  if this necessary so I think the company  recognized the strength of feeling on  the shop floor I think they recognize  the union its members were serious about  getting a decent payable this year but  also I think from the company's  perspective they recognize that their  work workers are in a unique situation  at the moment as they're trying to  address the cost living crisis that all  workers are facing across the economy so  I think the the negotiations were good  towards the end I think we got to a  position where we could recommend a deal  and obviously the the Reps have done a  fantastic job in negotiating a deal  alongside the union too so I think for  all parties it was good you know pay's  been delivered on time and it was a good  um a good end of the year you made an  interesting point there about the  approach of Rolls-Royce I mean obviously  Rolls-Royce Motors is owned by BMW of  Germany I mean unions and uh employers  are often seen as having quite a  collaborative relationship in in that  country do you think there's a lesson to  be learned here from the approach of  Rolls-Royce Motors  yeah I think ultimately we've got um  obviously there's a number of different  plants under the BMW group Banner in  this in this country you've got the  plant Oxford you've got plant Swindon  you've got Hans Hall and I think the  different plants have different cultures  I think certainly we've spent a long  time over the past year negotiating a  new recognition agreement that puts the  union a more even footing especially  when in terms of negotiating with the  company because we you know we need to  ensure that we negotiating from a  position of strength rather than  weakness and our members have kind of  responded to that so so yeah I think  there's probably things that we can  learn going forward from both parties  but again it's a positive outcome it's a  one-year deal you know so this time next  year we'll be negotiating again I think  we won't know at this moment in time  exactly we'll get economic content or  context will be but we're determined to  ensure that our Rolls-Royce members are  only the same as other car or workers in  the premium enter the sector so so yeah  I think it's it's positive work I think  the relationship with Rolls Royce at the  moment is good I think everyone's happy  that we've got a pay deal in place it's  going to be paid on time but again we'll  wait to see where we are around the sort  of September point and next year when we  submit the next claim but do you think  other employers could learn from how  Rolls-Royce Motors conducted th